<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    The chosen answer, revealed to be wrong.

    Set on the option's CardView by QuizActivity, which is why all four states
    are backgrounds rather than view properties: the selection model is
    "swap the background drawable", and these keep that contract while drawing
    the Ring-led design's hairline row instead of a 6dp stroked pill.

    THE FIRST LAYER IS LOAD-BEARING AND INVISIBLE. CardView calls
    setClipToOutline(true) in its constructor, and the outline is taken from
    the background - where LayerDrawable.getOutline() returns the FIRST layer
    that yields a non-empty one. With the hairline first, every row clipped
    its own text to a 1dp band and the options vanished. A full-bounds
    transparent rectangle in front of it gives the outline the row's real
    shape and costs nothing to draw.

    Then: the rule above the row, and the rail that carries the state. The
    last row's closing rule is a divider in activity_quiz.xml, because a
    per-row drawable cannot know which row is last.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item android:gravity="top" android:height="1dp">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="@color/stroke_card" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item
        android:gravity="center_vertical|start"
        android:start="4dp"
        android:width="5dp"
        android:height="22dp">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <corners android:radius="3dp" />
            <solid android:color="@color/difficulty_hard" />
        </shape>
    </item>

</layer-list>
